        <body>
  <h2>Student Rights & Discipline in Niagara Falls City School District</h2>

  <p>Niagara Falls City School District (NFCSD) is committed to providing a safe and supportive learning environment where all students thrive. Understanding your rights and responsibilities as a student, and as parents of students, is crucial for a positive and legally compliant school experience. This guide provides information on discipline, Manifestation Determination Reviews (MDRs), and due process rights for students with disabilities in NFCSD.</p>

  <h3>Understanding Discipline for Students with IEPs and 504 Plans</h3>

  <p>Disciplinary actions for students with Individualized Education Programs (IEPs) or 504 Plans are subject to specific protections under the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) and Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act. These protections are designed to ensure that students with disabilities are not unfairly punished for behaviors related to their disability. It's vital to know that NFCSD, like all districts in NYS, must adhere to these regulations.</p>

  <p><strong>Key Considerations:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Short-Term Suspensions (10 Days or Less):</strong>  NFCSD can suspend a student with an IEP or 504 plan for up to 10 consecutive school days for a violation of school rules, just like any other student. However, the school must still provide educational services if a pattern of short-term suspensions emerges that constitutes a change of placement (see below).</li>
    <li><strong>Change of Placement:</strong> A "change of placement" occurs when a student is (a) removed from their current educational placement for more than 10 consecutive school days; or (b) subjected to a series of removals that constitute a pattern because they total more than 10 school days in a school year; and because of factors such as the length of each removal, the total amount of time the student is removed, and the proximity of the removals to one another. NYS Part 201.6(a)(1) defines change of placement removals.</li>
   <li><strong>Educational Services During Suspension:</strong>  According to IDEA regulations, if a student with a disability is removed from their current placement for more than 10 school days in a school year, the school district must provide services to the extent determined necessary to enable the child to continue to participate in the general education curriculum, although in another setting, and to progress toward meeting the goals set out in the child’s IEP. See 34 CFR §300.530(d)(1).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Manifestation Determination Review (MDR): A Critical Safeguard</h3>

  <p>If a student with an Individualized Education Program (IEP) faces disciplinary action that could result in a suspension exceeding 10 school days (whether consecutive or cumulative creating a pattern that constitutes a change of placement), NFCSD <u>must</u> conduct a Manifestation Determination Review (MDR) within 10 school days of the decision to change the student’s placement.  This is a crucial safeguard to protect the rights of students with disabilities. This process determines if the student's behavior was caused by, or directly and substantially related to, their disability. It also assesses whether the behavior was a direct result of NFCSD's failure to implement the IEP.  34 CFR §300.530(e) details the requirements for MDRs.</p>

  <p><strong>The Manifestation Determination Process: A Step-by-Step Guide for Parents</strong></p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Notification:</strong> NFCSD must notify you of the decision to suspend your child for more than 10 days and inform you of the upcoming MDR meeting. This notice should include the date, time, location, and purpose of the meeting, as well as a list of the IEP team members who will be attending. Make sure you receive this notice in writing.</li>
    <li><strong>Information Gathering:</strong> Before the MDR meeting, gather all relevant information about your child's behavior and disability. This includes:
      <ul>
        <li>A copy of your child's current IEP and any recent evaluations.</li>
        <li>Any relevant medical or psychological reports.</li>
        <li>Your own observations of your child's behavior at home and in school.</li>
        <li>Any communication you've had with school staff regarding your child's behavior.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>The MDR Meeting:</strong> During the MDR meeting, the IEP team will review all relevant information, including the student's file, teacher observations, and any relevant evaluations.  The team must consider:
        <ul>
            <li>All relevant information in the student’s file</li>
            <li>The child’s IEP</li>
            <li>Any teacher observations</li>
            <li>Information provided by the parents</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Determining Manifestation:</strong> The IEP team will determine if the behavior in question was a manifestation of the student's disability.  According to IDEA, the behavior is a manifestation of the disability if:
      <ul>
        <li>The conduct in question was caused by or had a direct and substantial relationship to the child’s disability; <i>or</i></li>
        <li>The conduct in question was the direct result of the district’s failure to implement the IEP.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Outcomes:</strong>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>If the behavior <i>is</i> a manifestation of the disability or a failure in IEP implementation:</strong> The student returns to their placement unless otherwise agreed upon by the IEP team.  The IEP team will then conduct a Functional Behavioral Assessment (FBA), unless one has already been conducted, and develop or revise the IEP to address the behavior through a Behavior Intervention Plan (BIP).  The BIP should include strategies to prevent the behavior from recurring.  NFCSD must immediately take steps to remediate those deficiencies if the team finds that the conduct was the direct result of the school district's failure to implement the IEP.</li>
        <li><strong>If the behavior is <i>not</i> a manifestation of the disability:</strong> The student can be disciplined in the same manner as students without disabilities. However, the school must still provide educational services to the student during the suspension, as outlined in their IEP.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Document Everything:</strong> Keep detailed records of all communication with the school, including emails, letters, and phone calls.  Take notes during the MDR meeting and request a copy of the meeting minutes.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Suspension Advice for NFCSD Students: Know Your Rights</h3>

  <p>If you face a suspension in NFCSD, it's important to understand your rights. Students have the right to due process, which includes being informed of the charges against them and having an opportunity to present their side of the story. According to NYS Education Law §3214, schools must provide students with written notice of the charges against them, as well as an opportunity for an informal hearing. If you believe the suspension is unfair or that your rights have been violated, contact your parents or guardian immediately, and consider speaking with a school counselor or administrator. Documenting the situation and seeking support is crucial.</p>

  <p><strong>Key Due Process Rights:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Notice of Charges:</strong> You have the right to receive written notice of the specific charges against you, including the date, time, and location of the alleged incident.</li>
    <li><strong>Opportunity to be Heard:</strong> You have the right to present your side of the story to the school administration before a suspension is imposed. This includes the right to explain your actions and to present evidence in your defense.</li>
    <li><strong>Right to Question Witnesses:</strong> While not always guaranteed in informal hearings, you have the right to request the opportunity to question witnesses against you, depending on the severity of the potential suspension.</li>
    <li><strong>Right to Representation:</strong> For suspensions exceeding five days, you have the right to be represented by an attorney.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Parent Advocacy Tips for NFCSD Disciplinary Matters</h3>

  <p>As a parent, you are your child's best advocate. Here are some tips for navigating disciplinary matters in NFCSD:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Stay Informed:</strong> Familiarize yourself with NFCSD's student code of conduct and your child's IEP or 504 plan.</li>
    <li><strong>Communicate with the School:</strong> Maintain open communication with your child's teachers, counselors, and administrators.  Address concerns promptly and document all communication in writing.</li>
    <li><strong>Attend All Meetings:</strong> Attend all meetings related to your child's discipline, including MDR meetings, suspension hearings, and IEP team meetings.</li>
    <li><strong>Bring Support:</strong> Bring a friend, family member, or advocate to meetings for support and to help you take notes.</li>
    <li><strong>Request Documentation:</strong> Request copies of all relevant documents, including incident reports, witness statements, and meeting minutes.</li>
    <li><strong>Know Your Rights:</strong> Understand your child's rights under IDEA, Section 504, and New York State Education Law.</li>
    <li><strong>Consider Independent Evaluation:</strong> If you disagree with the school's evaluation of your child's behavior, you have the right to request an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) at public expense, under certain circumstances.</li>
    <li><strong>Seek Legal Advice:</strong> If you believe your child's rights have been violated, consult with a special education attorney.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Common District Pitfalls to Watch Out For</h3>

  <p>While NFCSD is committed to providing a fair and supportive learning environment, there are some common pitfalls that parents should be aware of:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Failure to Conduct Timely MDRs:</strong> NFCSD may sometimes fail to conduct MDRs within the required timeframe, which violates a student's due process rights.</li>
    <li><strong>Inadequate IEP Implementation:</strong> Disciplinary issues may arise from a failure to properly implement a student's IEP.  Ensure that NFCSD is providing all of the services and supports outlined in the IEP.</li>
    <li><strong>Lack of Communication:</strong> Poor communication between the school and parents can lead to misunderstandings and disagreements.  Establish clear lines of communication and document all interactions.</li>
    <li><strong>Bias and Discrimination:</strong> Be vigilant for any signs of bias or discrimination in the disciplinary process. Students with disabilities should not be treated differently from their non-disabled peers.</li>
     <li><strong>Improper Use of Restraint or Seclusion:</strong> New York State has strict regulations regarding the use of restraint and seclusion in schools. If you believe your child has been subjected to improper restraint or seclusion, contact an attorney immediately. Part 200.22 and 200.23 of the NYS Regulations detail the requirements for restraint and seclusion.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>FAQ: Discipline and Special Education in NFCSD</h3>

  <dl>
    <dt><strong>Q: What is a Functional Behavioral Assessment (FBA)?</strong></dt>
    <dd><strong>A:</strong> An FBA is a process used to identify the function or purpose of a student's behavior. It involves gathering information about the behavior, the triggers that precede it, and the consequences that follow it. The information gathered through the FBA is used to develop a Behavior Intervention Plan (BIP).</dd>

    <dt><strong>Q: What is a Behavior Intervention Plan (BIP)?</strong></dt>
    <dd><strong>A:</strong> A BIP is a plan that outlines strategies to address a student's challenging behavior. It is based on the findings of the FBA and includes proactive strategies to prevent the behavior from occurring, as well as reactive strategies to manage the behavior when it does occur. It must be data driven and have measurable goals.</dd>

    <dt><strong>Q: Can NFCSD suspend my child for behaviors related to their disability?</strong></dt>
    <dd><strong>A:</strong> NFCSD can suspend your child for behaviors related to their disability, but only after conducting a Manifestation Determination Review. If the behavior is determined to be a manifestation of the disability, the school must take steps to address the behavior through the IEP process.</dd>

    <dt><strong>Q: What if I disagree with the outcome of the Manifestation Determination Review?</strong></dt>
    <dd><strong>A:</strong> If you disagree with the outcome of the MDR, you have the right to challenge the decision through due process. This may involve filing a complaint with the New York State Education Department or requesting a due process hearing.</dd>

    <dt><strong>Q: Where can I find NFCSD's student code of conduct?</strong></dt>
    <dd><strong>A:</strong> You can find NFCSD's student code of conduct on the district's website or by contacting the school administration.  It's important to review the code of conduct to understand the rules and expectations for student behavior.</dd>
  </dl>

</body>
